<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Fritz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1654">1654</a>–1725</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Fritz was a Bohemian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary who worked among the Omagua and other peoples of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> from 1686. He founded dozens of missions along the river, resisted Portuguese slave raids and claims to the region, and drew the first reasonably accurate map of the Amazon, published in 1707. He died at the mission of Jeberos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1725.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-fritz.jpg" alt="Samuel Fritz" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ELRAFA67 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LA_PLAZA_CENTRAL.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
